<h2>The situation</h2>
<p>A seed-stage B2B software company had found traction. The founders were strong builders and the small engineering team shipped quickly, but the things that matter at the next stage were falling behind. Architecture choices were made in chat and forgotten, the release process depended on one person, there was no shared view of reliability, and enterprise prospects were sending security questionnaires nobody had time to answer well. A Series A process was on the horizon, and technical diligence would follow.</p>
<p>The company did not yet need, or want to pay for, a full-time CTO. It needed senior technical leadership for part of each week, with a clear plan for handing over.</p>

<h2>What had to be true</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>A roadmap the team believed</strong>, connecting product goals to platform work and technical debt.</li>
<li><strong>Decisions written down</strong>, so they can be reviewed, challenged and learned by new hires.</li>
<li><strong>Delivery and reliability measured</strong>, as signals for improvement rather than targets to game.</li>
<li><strong>Security that stands up to scrutiny</strong>, with controls and evidence rather than promises.</li>
<li><strong>A team that outlasts the engagement</strong>: senior hires and habits that keep working after hand-off.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Architecture</h2>
<p>Here the architecture was as much the operating model as the system. The technical work rested on a light set of leadership practices, each producing an artefact the team and investors could see.</p>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-stack">
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Direction</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Technical roadmap<em>tied to company plan</em></span><span>Investor narrative<em>technology story</em></span><span>Build vs buy<em>explicit choices</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Decisions</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">ADRs<em>in the repository</em></span><span>Architecture review<em>regular, short</em></span><span>Tech debt register<em>ranked by risk</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Delivery</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>CI/CD<em>automated releases</em></span><span>Infrastructure as code<em>reviewable environments</em></span><span>DORA metrics<em>throughput and stability</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Reliability and security</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>SLOs<em>key customer journeys</em></span><span class="hl">Security controls<em>SOC 2 readiness</em></span><span>Incident reviews<em>blameless</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>People</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Hiring plan<em>senior roles first</em></span><span>Career framework<em>simple levels</em></span><span>Successor<em>full-time leader</em></span></div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Operating model: each layer produces something written and reviewable, from roadmap to hiring plan.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Architecture decision records followed the lightweight format Michael Nygard described: context, decision and consequences, kept with the code<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. How I use them in a fractional role is in <a href="/insights/architecture-decision-records-fractional-cto/">architecture decision records for fractional CTOs</a>.</p>

<h2>The data flow, end to end</h2>
<p>The cadence mattered more than any single document. Each cycle followed the same loop:</p>
<figure class="dgm">
<ol class="dgm-flow g3">
<li><b>Assess</b><span>Review the stack, delivery pipeline, incidents and open risks</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Decide</b><span>Write ADRs for significant choices; review with the engineers affected</span></li>
<li><b>Plan</b><span>Update the roadmap and debt register against company priorities</span></li>
<li><b>Deliver</b><span>Team ships; pipeline produces delivery and reliability data automatically</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Measure</b><span>Discuss DORA metrics and SLO error budgets as improvement signals</span></li>
<li><b>Report</b><span>Short update for founders and board on progress, risks and hiring</span></li>
</ol>
<figcaption>A repeating leadership loop: decisions and measurements feed the next plan.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Delivery was tracked with DORA's software delivery metrics, which cover throughput and instability. DORA itself warns against turning them into blanket targets, so they were used per service and only to find bottlenecks<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>. See <a href="/insights/dora-metrics-ai-assisted-engineering/">DORA metrics for AI-assisted engineering</a>. Reliability used a small number of SLOs on the journeys customers cared about, with an agreed policy for what happens when an error budget is spent<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>.</p>

<h2>The hard part: adding structure without slowing the team</h2>
<p>A fast seed team is right to be suspicious of process. Every practice had to earn its place by saving more time than it cost. That meant:</p>
<ul>
<li>ADRs only for decisions that are expensive to reverse, written in under an hour, not for every library choice.</li>
<li>Automating the evidence: delivery metrics from the pipeline, access reviews from the identity provider, change history from pull requests.</li>
<li>Sequencing security work against real demand. The SOC 2 trust services criteria (security, availability, processing integrity, confidentiality and privacy) gave the structure<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>, with security controls first because every enterprise questionnaire asked about them. More in <a href="/insights/soc2-for-ai-startups/">SOC 2 for AI startups</a>.</li>
<li>Hiring senior engineers who could own areas, not just add capacity, so leadership could be handed over rather than borrowed.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout"><p>A fractional CTO succeeds when the role becomes unnecessary. The test is whether decisions, metrics and hiring keep happening well in the weeks you are not there.</p></div>

<h2>How the engagement ran</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-lanes">
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Founders</small><ol><li>Set company priorities</li><li>Approve hiring plan</li><li class="hl">Own investor narrative</li></ol></div>
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Fractional CTO</small><ol><li>Run architecture reviews</li><li class="hl">Write and coach ADRs</li><li>Lead senior hiring</li></ol></div>
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Engineering team</small><ol><li>Propose and record decisions</li><li>Own delivery and on-call</li><li>Join interview loops</li></ol></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Clear ownership: founders set direction, the CTO sets technical standards, the team owns delivery.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-phases">
<div><small>Phase 1</small><b>Assess</b><ul><li>Stack and pipeline review</li><li>Risk and debt register</li><li>Quick wins</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 2</small><b>Foundations</b><ul><li>Roadmap and ADRs</li><li>CI/CD and infrastructure as code</li><li>Core security controls</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 3</small><b>Scale</b><ul><li>DORA metrics and SLOs</li><li>SOC 2 readiness</li><li>Senior hires</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 4</small><b>Hand off</b><ul><li>Diligence pack</li><li>Full-time leader onboarding</li><li>Advisory follow-up</li></ul></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Phased leadership: assess, lay foundations, scale, then hand over to a permanent leader.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>Key decisions and trade-offs</h2>
<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Alternative</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Fractional CTO first</td><td>Hire full-time CTO immediately</td><td>Senior judgement now, while the company learns what role it needs</td></tr>
<tr><td>ADRs in the repository</td><td>Wiki pages or chat threads</td><td>Versioned with code, reviewed like code, found by new hires</td></tr>
<tr><td>Metrics as signals</td><td>Metrics as targets</td><td>Avoids gaming; focuses on bottlenecks</td></tr>
<tr><td>Security controls before certification</td><td>Rush to an audit</td><td>Real controls and evidence first; audit when customers require it</td></tr>
<tr><td>Senior owners before headcount</td><td>Hire for capacity</td><td>Creates leaders who can take over from the fractional role</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>What I'd do again, and differently</h2>
<p>Again: write decisions down from the first week and keep the cadence predictable, even when the week is busy. Differently: start the search for the permanent engineering leader earlier. Good senior hires take longer than any technical workstream, and the hand-off is only as strong as the person receiving it.</p>
<p>If your team is at a similar stage, the <a href="/fractional-cto/">fractional CTO</a> page describes how I work, <a href="/insights/when-do-you-need-a-fractional-cto/">when you need one</a> covers the signals, or <a href="/#assess">analyse your challenge</a> and bring the result to a call.</p>
